1. **GAP — mixed-residual recursion lacks the block theorem needed for exhaustion.**

   The master says:

   > “At a mixed residual, this operation is performed separately on each label block.”

   and clause 4 says:

   > “mixed residuals are first split into their Galois-stable label blocks, and clauses 2–3 are applied independently to those blocks.”

   But the hypotheses never state the necessary intermediate result: for every label \(r\), its class is represented by a monic block polynomial whose polygon is a single side and whose residual is \(c\,r^k\), with the block polynomials multiplying to the parent.

   This matters when the whole residual is inseparable but contains a simple factor. Clause 2 cannot apply because it assumes the whole residual is separable; clause 3 applies only to repeated factors. Root-class disjointness from S1.7 does not itself provide the required block development, residual purity, window/cap assertions, or accounting identity. Consequently the final well-founded induction has no displayed terminal or transition case for a simple companion class inside a mixed residual. This is precisely the substantive content of source ANNEX-LEMMA HE7-13′, but that content is not installed as a master hypothesis.

